  "slug": "arterial-domain-protein-2fbe",
  "summary": "Five preprints link mechanical cues, ECM and signaling pathways to vascular and lymphatic phenotypes across development, aging and injury.",
  "synthesis": "The preprints converge loosely on how mechanical and matrix-related cues specify or maintain vessel and tissue function. In aging mouse collecting lymphatics, reduced SRF-driven contractility rather than smooth muscle cell loss underlies dilation and impaired drainage. Transmural compression impairs lymphatic endothelial barrier integrity and promotes inflammation via a Piezo1-CaMKII Ca2+ axis. In zebrafish choroid plexus, hemodynamic forces, klf2a/klf2b and Yap1/Wwtr1-dependent epithelial-endothelial Vegfab-Vegfr2 signaling establish fenestrated endothelial identity. Two developmental mouse studies concern matrix: neural crest Tmem2 loss causes cleft palate with HA accumulation, hypoxia and vascular defects, and stromal Ilk deletion disrupts fibronectin expression and renal tissue integrity within a defined time window. Together they emphasize mechanotransduction and ECM as determinants of vascular phenotype.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
